<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Broiler.VM.Composition.PolyglotCli</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Broiler.VM.Composition.PolyglotCli</AssemblyName>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>

    <!-- NOT PACKABLE, AND THE REASON IS THE ONE EVERY ROOT HERE GIVES. `docs/compositions.md`
         section 1 advertises nothing, and this root is the one a reader is most likely to expect
         to be shipped, because it is the tool the repository owner asked for: one command that
         runs both input profiles. It is not shipped. The JavaScript surface admits no async
         function, no module of its own, no Proxy and no BigInt; the WebAssembly surface admits no
         import, no text format, no vector, no GC, no exception, no thread and no memory64. A tool
         advertised as a polyglot host would be claiming both, and neither claim is one this
         checkout can make. -->
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>

    <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
    <TrimMode>full</TrimMode>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <UseSystemResourceKeys>true</UseSystemResourceKeys>

    <TrimmerSingleWarn>false</TrimmerSingleWarn>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    THE FIRST IMAGE IN THIS REPOSITORY THAT COMPOSES TWO PRODUCT PROFILES.

    `docs/roadmap.md` section 14 has asked since VM-3 for a catalog test over two profiles, and
    section 16 records that a composition hosting two profiles does not close a gate until that
    test exists. Until now the only two-profile composition in the checkout was
    `Broiler.VM.Composition.Workbench`, whose two profiles are the fixture profiles
    `Com.Example.Calculator` and `Com.Example.Ledger` - written by this repository, for this
    repository, to be composed. This root composes `broiler.javascript` and
    `broiler.webassembly`: two profiles with different payload formats, different manifests,
    different diagnostic vocabularies and different execution models, neither of which was written
    with the other in mind.

    WHAT IT DOES NOT DO IS CLOSE THAT ROW. Section 14 asks for a profile composed beside a
    DELIBERATELY ADVERSE neighbour, showing the neighbour's hard maxima do not reach it while its
    adopted defaults do, and this root writes no such test. Section 16 says a second product
    profile turns the argument into one with two real parties and "does not make either pass
    earlier", which is exactly this root's position.

    WHAT THE REFERENCE SET IS, AND WHY IT IS SIX. Three core projects, the JavaScript profile and
    its lowering, and the WebAssembly profile. The JavaScript format assembly arrives transitively
    through the profile and is in the image without being named here, which is what the register's
    sibling column exists to record. The WebAssembly profile has no format sibling and no lowering
    at all, because its payload is a bare module produced by an external toolchain.

    RULE N2 FORBIDS A CROSS-FAMILY EDGE AND EXCEPTS EXACTLY THIS. No project in one
    `Broiler.VM.Profile.<Language>` family may reference a project in another, and no project
    outside every family may reference into one - "except a composition root, which A12 bounds
    instead". A12's bound is three core projects plus one or more profile assemblies, and this
    root has that and nothing else. So the two families still cannot see each other; a composition
    can see both.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.VM.Abstractions\Broiler.VM.Abstractions.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.VM.Binary\Broiler.VM.Binary.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.VM.Runtime\Broiler.VM.Runtime.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript\Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript.Compiler\Broiler.VM.Profile.JavaScript.Compiler.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Broiler.VM.Profile.WebAssembly\Broiler.VM.Profile.WebAssembly.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
